    <node id="Y. Wang">
      <data key="d0">Y. Wang</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Y. Wang is an author and researcher recognized for significant contributions in the fields of prompt retrieval, retrieval techniques, and language models. Notably, Y. Wang’s work includes studies focused on universal prompt retrieval and self-regenerative retrieval techniques, demonstrating a commitment to advancing methodologies that enhance information retrieval systems.

In addition to prompt retrieval, Y. Wang is involved in research that explores learning representations and factual knowledge boundaries within language models. This aspect of Y. Wang’s work emphasizes the importance of understanding how knowledge is represented in AI systems, potentially affecting their efficacy in real-world applications.

Y. Wang has co-authored influential papers, including one on prompt-guided retrieval augmentation and another centered on knowledge graph prompting for document-based question answering. These collaborative efforts highlight Y. Wang's dedication to integrating theoretical insights with practical applications in emerging technologies.

Furthermore, Y. Wang is engaged in the domain of dialogue systems, contributing to research that examines retrieval models within this context. The contributions to the understanding of large language models in dialogue are particularly relevant given the rapid integration of such technologies across various platforms and applications. Collectively, Y. Wang’s research encompasses a broad spectrum of interests aimed at enhancing both the understanding and functionality of retrieval systems in artificial intelligence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58dcb3df5feab79a2a854dedb0e04591&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24bb482395464f98090b296c7be701c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-237b532b137d16ca46f782e4e88d372d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50fab934716e3d9351f065107bc18294&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84e65b4bb00eb8e1046ad99348354b03&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-515daf26b5c5997844fc67ed1b2ebb91</data>
